# Spring Cache (write-through)

Use Spring Cache against Redis. Do not keep cache in process memory.

## Retrieval and mutation

- `@Cacheable` on reads: Redis first, then DB.
- `@CachePut` or `@CacheEvict` on POST/PUT/DELETE so every pod sees the update immediately.
- Cache keys must be stable and explicit (`unless`, `key`, cache names). Do not invent a refresh endpoint.

```java
// ❌ BAD — pod-local cache
private static final Map<Long, Account> CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

// ✅ GOOD
@Cacheable(cacheNames = "accounts", key = "#id")
public Account getAccount(Long id) { return repository.findById(id).orElseThrow(); }

@CachePut(cacheNames = "accounts", key = "#result.id")
public Account updateAccount(Account account) { return repository.save(account); }

@CacheEvict(cacheNames = "accounts", key = "#id")
public void deleteAccount(Long id) { repository.deleteById(id); }
```

## Entities

JPA entities stored in Redis must implement `Serializable`.
